WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.562
OT telemetry is observable data produced by industrial systems, networks, devices,

00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:06.184
applications, and physical processes.

00:00:06.184 --> 00:00:11.457
It is the evidence that helps teams understand what exists, what is communicating,

00:00:11.457 --> 00:00:14.297
what changed, and how the operation responded.

00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:18.759
Telemetry can include network packets and flow records, device health events,

00:00:18.759 --> 00:00:22.409
controller status, process values, alarms, user authentication, application logs,

00:00:22.409 --> 00:00:25.248
remote-access sessions, file activity, and configuration changes.

00:00:25.248 --> 00:00:28.899
Each source describes a different layer of the environment.

00:00:28.899 --> 00:00:32.144
Raw telemetry is not the same as visibility.

00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:37.417
A record becomes useful when it has reliable time, asset identity, operational meaning,

00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:41.068
sufficient retention, and an owner who understands the source.

00:00:41.068 --> 00:00:45.935
A device address or alarm code without context may be technically accurate

00:00:45.935 --> 00:00:47.963
but difficult to act upon.

00:00:47.963 --> 00:00:52.019
Security teams use telemetry to build inventory, establish expected behavior,

00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:55.670
design detections, investigate anomalies, reconstruct incidents, and verify architecture.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.943
Operations and engineering teams use many of the same records for troubleshooting, maintenance,

00:01:00.943 --> 00:01:02.971
quality, reliability, and performance analysis.

00:01:02.971 --> 00:01:04.999
Collection must respect OT constraints.

00:01:04.999 --> 00:01:09.867
Passive network monitoring and existing logs are often preferred near sensitive devices.

00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:11.489
New agents, active queries,

00:01:11.489 --> 00:01:17.168
and configuration changes should be tested and approved so visibility does not introduce latency,

00:01:17.168 --> 00:01:18.790
instability, or unsupported behavior.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.441
A telemetry program asks practical questions: what activity matters,

00:01:22.441 --> 00:01:27.714
which source can observe it, what is missing, how trustworthy is the data,

00:01:27.714 --> 00:01:30.148
and who responds when collection fails?

00:01:30.148 --> 00:01:32.581
Strong telemetry does not guarantee security,

00:01:32.581 --> 00:01:36.232
but it replaces assumptions with evidence that security, operations,

00:01:36.232 --> 00:01:38.260
and engineering can interpret together.
